text:
reena: Heads-up: found an awkward behavior in the Go Generator SDK during an onboarding workshop. When the HTTP handler spawns background workers that stream generation and the incoming request cancels, the SDK's stream object sometimes blocks indefinitely if auth refresh happens mid-stream. Repro in the workshop sample app with ~200 concurrent simulated users.

mike: Can you paste the minimal snippet?

reena: It's similar to this simplified pattern from the workshop sample: 
```go
func serve(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
  go func() {
    ctx := r.Context()
    stream, err := client.Generator.Stream(ctx, req)
    if err != nil { return }
    for stream.Next() {
      // forward tokens to websocket/client, but sometimes client disconnects
    }
    // we never call stream.Close() here in the detach pattern
  }()
  w.WriteHeader(200)
}
```

ty: Two problems: detached goroutine + assuming ctx cancel cascades cleanup + auth refresh concurrency. The SDK's streaming code should probably return an explicit Close() and a Done() channel to avoid resource leaks when callers are sloppy.

reena: In the workshop we updated the quickstart live to show one-line fix (wrap with WithCancel and defer cancel, and ensure draining stream.Next() until false). I filed GH issue: "go sdk: docs & sample - stream must be closed and drained when using request contexts".

mike: Let's also add a short note about auth rotations: when token refresh occurs mid-request, transient send errors occur and our internal retry may hold the send loop open until success, which keeps file descriptors. For now, add a best-practice doc note to use short-lived contexts for background jobs or explicitly cancel on client disconnect.

doc-bot: New PR suggested: devex/docs#512 - "Go generator quickstart: explicit stream cleanup"

reena: I'll push the sample app fix and a tiny FAQ. Also want to propose an SDK change: make Stream implement io.Closer and add Stream.Done() chan struct{} so callers can select on ctx.Done() vs stream.Done() and ensure proper termination. Thoughts?

ty: API change ok but we should avoid breaking existing code. Consider adding methods non-breaking first: Add Close(ctx) and Done() but keep existing behavior until major version bump. Add finalizer logs in debug builds and metrics for leaked streams.

mike: Agree. Immediate 3 actions: 1) Update docs and sample app (prio), 2) Add monitoring (open_stream_count), 3) SDK incremental change (Done()+Close(ctx)) behind feature flag.

reena: Thanks all — PR coming for docs + sample. I'll open an RFC for the SDK method additions.
